| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| Threshold | 1024 | 1024 | 1.000000 | 128.000000 | 255.000000 | 127.000000 |
| Diffusion | 1024 | 512 | 0.500000 | 128.000000 | 127.500000 | -0.500000 |
\image(figures/floyd_steinberg_comparison.svg)
// result_end
\caption(A uniform128 patch becomes all white under independent thresholding. The raster diffusion result mixes black and white; the table reports its actual density and mean error.)
\alttext(Two native32-by32 pixel panels compare a completely white threshold result with the black-and-white raster pattern produced by diffusion on the same uniform gray input.)

Visual artifacts depend on image content and scan direction. The primary research
paper cited above discusses patterning, worm-like structures and edge effects in
traditional error diffusion. Our fixed raster scan is not a serpentine variant,
a color halftoner, a noise-driven method or their improved threshold-modulation
algorithm. No claim that it is always best-looking or faster is needed here.

> Boundary loss and finite-image tone balance

For a pixel p let e_p be its actual quantization error and a_p the sum of weights
whose destinations lie outside the image. In exact arithmetic, summing every pixel
cancels internal transfers, leaving

$$
\sum_p\mathrm{input}_p-\sum_p\mathrm{output}_p
=\sum_p a_p e_p.
$$
\alttext(The total input intensity minus total output intensity equals the sum of each error times the fraction discarded outside the image, in exact arithmetic.)

The right-hand side is signed: positive loss means the output sum is smaller;
negative loss means it is larger. For floating-point computation the native
`balance_residual` is input sum minus output sum minus that signed boundary term.
A small residual checks this accounting, not perceptual fidelity. It is not a promise
that image means match. A single pixel of127.5 becomes zero and discards its entire
127.5 error, providing a direct counterexample to exact finite-image preservation.

The next controls hold the intended value at64 while changing square size. Density
is measured against64/255. Neither monotone improvement with size nor equality at
every finite size is assumed. Work counts include four direction slots per pixel;
only valid destinations cause an update.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/floyd_steinberg_balance.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/grayscale_images.hpp>
#include <limits>
#include <print>
namespace dm=datamunge;
int main(){
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  std::vector<double> index,density,pixels,slots,updates;std::vector<std::string> labels;
  std::print("| side | pixels | white pixels | white fraction | mean error | boundary loss | balance residual | actual updates |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(std::size_t side:{1u,2u,4u,8u,16u,32u,64u}){const std::vector<std::vector<double>> input(side,std::vector<double>(side,64.));const auto result=dm::algorithms::floyd_steinberg_work(input);const auto v=dm::algorithms::dither_region_stats(input,result.output,0,side);const auto&w=result.work;
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {:.6f} | {:.6f} | {:.9f} | {:.3e} | {} |\n",side,w.pixels,w.white,v.white_fraction,v.mean_error,w.boundary_loss,w.balance_residual,w.updates);
    index.push_back(double(index.size()));density.push_back(double(v.white_fraction));pixels.push_back(double(w.pixels));slots.push_back(double(w.neighbor_slots));updates.push_back(double(w.updates));labels.push_back(std::to_string(side));
  }
  auto tone=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();tone.points(index,density,"Actual white fraction",{22,163,74},5).lines({0.,6.},{64./255.,64./255.},"Intended64/255",{220,38,38},1.5).x_tick_labels(labels).ylim(0,.28).title("Uniform64: actual binary density").xlab("Square side (categorical)").ylab("White fraction").legend({"Measured","64/255"},{{22,163,74},{220,38,38}});
  auto work=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();work.lines(pixels,slots,"Slots",{220,38,38},2).lines(pixels,updates,"Updates",{37,99,235},2).title("Attempted slots and valid updates").xlab("Input pixels").ylab("Actual counted operations").legend({"Four slots per pixel","In-bounds updates"},{{220,38,38},{37,99,235}});
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2).add(tone).add(work).size(1100,540).title("Finite edges affect tone; each pixel has constant stencil work").fit_panels().save_svg("build/floyd_steinberg_balance.svg");
}
