Checked controls: ragged rejected true; NaN rejected before quota true; whole cap31 rejected true; cap32 steps 4; neighbor arithmetic overflow rejected true.
Ownership: source unchanged after call true; owned input independent after caller edit true.
Cutoff127.75 output 0; exact128 output 255; finite maximum single-pixel output 255.
Virtual size_t-max top-right center: right false; down-left true; down true; down-right false.
One-pixel tone control: input 127.5; output 0; discarded 127.5; residual 0.
\image(figures/floyd_steinberg_controls.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Ragged/nonfinite sources reject; a two-by-two trace needs an upper admission bound32. Actual arithmetic overflow is distinct from input finiteness, and successful trace storage is independent.)
\alttext(Three native boxes show whole trace quota31 versus32, nonfinite-source and update-overflow checks, and independent owned input storage.)

The ordinary by-value overload copies an lvalue before its body validates; an rvalue
can transfer its storage. Work and trace overloads validate read-only input before
copying. No numerical API mutates the caller's source, and failures return no partial
output. Empty and zero-width rectangular images retain their shapes.

All five programs are complete editable C++ sources and call the shared native kernel,
fixtures, threshold baseline and statistics helpers. There is no external dataset.
Verification preserves original pixel decisions and exact floating-point update bits,
checks small grids against independent rational gathering, uses higher-precision
conditional controls, replays actual update evidence, tests signed edge balance,
full-width geometry, quotas and ownership, and runs address/undefined-behavior
sanitizers. Native figures are saved locally and copied into the report.
